    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d157">

                <pb facs="0246" n="234" sameAs="frus:frus1943v03/pg234"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/1279: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Finland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">McClintock</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Helsinki</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-02-09T14:00:00+02:00">February 9, 1943—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 9—1:46 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>226. We are told that the Finnish language broadcast from Moscow<anchor n="83"
                        xml:id="d157-f1"/> last week stated that Helsinki would not be bombed by the
                    Soviet Air Force as the Americans had interceded on the behalf of Finland and
                    were arranging peace between the Soviet Union and this country.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">McClintock</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d157-f1" n="83" xml:id="d157-f1-content">
                <p>By the Government-controlled Radio Moscow.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
